  "summary": "Recent preprints use diffusion, morphometric and resting-state MRI to link brain variability and structure to age, sex, cognition and clinical traits.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is human MRI phenotyping that relates individual differences in brain structure or intrinsic signal dynamics to age, cognition and clinical status. Several preprints focus on moment-to-moment signal properties rather than mean activation. These include resting BOLD variability and its brain-behavior covariance in adults with dyslexia, reduced resting entropy in right superior temporal gyrus in hearing loss, and developmental reorganization of intrinsic dynamics tied to contextual inhibitory control in youths aged 8–18. A related study shows structure-function coupling is state-dependent: it is strongest during movie-watching and weakest under working memory load. Structural work spans the lifespan. It reports nonlinear, posterior-weighted cerebellar atrophy with midlife vulnerability in 1,371 adults, and choroid plexus volume increasing with age and larger in males across 700 manually segmented scans. In adolescents, ACC sulcal symmetry relates to the balance of proactive versus reactive control. Bullying victimisation is associated with altered medial occipital white matter maturation, which moderates links to psychotic-like experiences. Most samples outside the aging and choroid plexus datasets are modest (52–101 participants).",
